  "body": "**This first writer now uses the zero-step encoder from #3104. Keep it in draft for the required safety-tier review.** Later feature drafts still need their own safety review before they can introduce machining behavior. A stale controller quality can change the effective finish, pass count and offsets.\n\nThis change builds on the machine template in #2948 and the standalone encoder in #3104. These branches are in a fork, so this PR targets upstream main. Review [the first-writer safety adoption above its integration parent](https://github.com/qsmits/Camcraft/compare/1ea87ebac0204f3613c3d2549692b4108f620f66...025ed4760e592c3401859a66285457021a98341b).\n\n## Change\n\nThe desktop Post command and cam.post write a native Agie job directory with linked ISO, SBR and SBL files. CamCraft writes the nominal contour in Rust. The setup requests a resident quality and leaves every work with zero erosion steps. The post validates the work before it creates the destination. It publishes all three files together and preserves an existing directory.\n\nThe supported case is one vertical rectangular punch in 20 mm stock. Select the Classic 2S template from #2948. Enter the AGIE_QN2 condition for Berco Cut 0.25 mm wire and cold die steel. Select the 1.0 mm separation cut explicitly. The template starts without an installed condition. A cut needs 0.4 mm clearance at each stock face and 3 mm of straight face on each side of the entry. Other contours, tapers, skims, custom X/Y travel limits and additional workpieces are refused.\n\n## Reference material for review\n\nThe contributor's team wrote [Agie-CAM at 11b1bb9](https://github.com/qsmits/Agie-CAM/tree/11b1bb9ef2c752b94b6fefc1f0814acd2e030c42) and released it under the MIT license. This draft retains the license and copyright notice. The implementation reference documents observed controller examples for parts of the JEScript sequence and the resident technology selection. The published guides linked on #2945 give the file roles and some interface arguments. They do not document the full sequence and all defaults. Please review that source against the project rules before merge.\n\nThe controller supplies its own runtime and technology table. This PR includes the writer and original synthetic examples and expected output. It includes no controller image, vendor post, runtime library or technology database.\n\n## Control verification\n\nOn the corrected writer, the normal CLI produced the same three CCSEP20 files as the independently verified import in #3104. Only the output stem was changed from the PUNCH40 example. The copy of the actual control showed the closed punch at zero steps. FORMALCHECK blocked it until the operator assigns technology. See [the file hashes and native work details](https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/pull/3104#issuecomment-7055077111). The native bytes were tested once in that replay. No physical cut was made.\n\nOn successive imports, select the newly created piece. Match its ISO, start hole and zero steps to the intended setup. Verify the effective wire, height, quality and offsets before assigning TECCUT. Run the control's graphic and formal checks on the resulting steps.\n\nOn the earlier writer, the control plotted a rough pass and three trims after automatic generation. The measured offsets were 0.245, 0.155, 0.140 and 0.134 mm. That historical result does not qualify an automatic release on this or another installation.\n\nThe synthetic example runs from the repository root:\n```sh\ncargo run -p camcraft-app --bin camcraft-cli -- script examples/agie/punch.camscript\n```\nUse a new PUNCH40 destination.\n\n## Final head checks\n\nAt a08c802b6, all five writer tests, four standalone encoder tests and ten application integration tests passed. The normal PUNCH40 script reproduced the previous guarded ISO, SBR and SBL. A repeat export refused to replace them. Both PUNCH40 and the CCSEP20 variant returned `working_steps_generated=false` and the controller review.\n\n```sh\ncargo test -p camcraft-post --lib agie::tests::\ncargo test -p camcraft-post --test agie_safe_setup\ncargo test -p camcraft-app --test agie_native\n```\n\nPinning tests include `script_post_reports_the_required_control_review_and_zero_working_steps`, `stock_origin_and_fractional_setup_coordinates_are_refused_before_publication`, `the_native_files_match_the_control_checked_rectangular_punch` and `qn2_requires_the_rough_wire_size_in_the_mounted_condition`.\n\nThe earlier guarded head was 5bdbb5fe4. It includes the upstream diagnostic fix from #3092 and a dedicated recorded-stream suite for the Agie dispatcher. The single-file post entry point must refuse the Agie dispatcher. Each of 11 input streams checks the diagnostic, the failure exit code and the absence of NC output. The suite inventory also requires this post to have its own tests.\n\nOn 5bdbb5fe4 the recorded suite passed 12/12 tests. The dispatcher suite passed 11/11. All nine default-machine regression tests and all 19 native tests passed. The normal CLI and an independent Rust caller reproduced the checked setup files and the matching ISO. Both duplicate exports preserved all files. Against upstream 211105042, the full plan selects 1,017 quick steps and 251 reached heavy steps. These two full gates did not run on that head.\n\n```sh\ncargo test -p camcraft-post --test script_agie_native --test recorded_streams\ncargo test -p camcraft-app --test default_machine_missing\n```\n\nThe final head 025ed4760 adds cross-post tests. The plasma suite now runs the direct Agie dispatcher and requires its native-directory refusal. The single-file smoothing test uses the same native-post predicate as the workspace. A mounted, otherwise valid Agie punch with smoothing enabled must report the setup-option error and create no directory. The existing native test checks all three default-option files against their expected bytes.\n\nAll 48 targeted checks passed on 025ed4760: seven plasma tests, seven smoothing tests, 11 native app tests, 11 dispatcher tests and 12 common recorded-stream tests. No full quick or reached-heavy result is claimed on this revision.\n\n```sh\ncargo test -p camcraft-app --test plasma_post_refusals --test smoothing --test agie_native\ncargo test -p camcraft-post --test script_agie_native --test recorded_streams\n```\n\nThe compiled desktop picker and receipt were checked locally on the safety-adoption source a08c802b6. The picker identifies Ra as requested. The receipt shows the ISO, threading-hole and working-step checks, the TECCUT assignment and the required offsets. Updated screenshots remain local for review.\n\n## Earlier desktop Post dialog\n\nThese pictures show the earlier writer. The corrected picker labels Ra as requested. The corrected receipt reports zero working steps and the required check of effective quality.\n\n| Before (Generic ISO) | After (native Agie) |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![Generic ISO Post dialog](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qsmits/Camcraft/625ef345a047980ab6974df99ea8ca2ac5f783b2/before.png) | ![Native Agie Post dialog](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qsmits/Camcraft/625ef345a047980ab6974df99ea8ca2ac5f783b2/after.png) |\n\nEffort: high\nChangelog: The Wire EDM desktop and script workflows can post a supported Classic Cut 2S punch as a native zero-step Agie setup directory for control review.\n\nFixes #2945\n\n\n\n\n",
